            <ArticleTitle>Walter Mercer's (1891-1971) contribution to the surgical treatment of low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>108-14</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0967772014565566</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In 1936, Walter Mercer described a new method for the operative treatment of patients with spondylolisthesis. Using a transabdominal approach in two patients he inserted iliac crest bone graft into the intervertebral disc. His publication in the Edinburgh Medical Journal caused a furore as the levels operated on did not reflect the description and one of the two patients died post-operatively. However, Mercer continued to promote the operation in his textbooks. The anterior approach to the lumbar spine is now performed routinely. This paper explores Mercer's contribution to anterior spinal surgery.</AbstractText>
